          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The net primarily symbolizes the kingdom or gospel gathering people, especially through evangelism, with final eschatological sorting; contextually it can also signify hostile snares, oppressive conquest, or divine capture for judgment.\",\"book_summary\":\"The net is an enclosure wielded by a power to draw living peoples from the symbolic sea into an irreversible harvest or judgment. The kingdom’s gospel, divinely used armies, and wicked traps are treated as different instances of this general pattern.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The net is the kingdom or gospel’s instrument for gathering people, ultimately including judgment and separation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The net is any power’s instrument of collective capture and removal from the sea of peoples for consecration, sorting, or destruction.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book makes capture by a power for irreversible disposition the governing category and reclassifies evangelistic gathering as only the kingdom’s instance of it. It places military capture and wicked snares within the same unified symbolism, whereas the consensus treats these as context-dependent alternatives to the primary evangelistic meaning.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction: the book expressly retains the gospel or kingdom net and its final sorting. The material difference is that it no longer treats evangelistic ingathering as the symbol’s central or dominant identity and universalizes the judgment-and-capture structure more strongly than the consensus does.\",\"rationale\":\"The central consensus referent is preserved in Matthew 13, but the book abstracts it into a broader theory covering kingdom gathering, imperial or military capture, divine judgment, and wicked entrapment. Those additional uses were already recognized among the consensus alternatives, so the entry is not novel; its refinement lies chiefly in unifying and reprioritizing them under one power-and-harvest definition. Claims such as “never ransomed” and the dragnet/devoted-thing wordplay further sharpen the book’s boundary beyond the consensus.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed passages establish several distinct uses of nets—Babylonian conquest, divine capture, kingdom gathering and final separation, and abundant fishing—but do not by themselves prove that all nets share one universal essence of irreversible consecration or destruction. The claims about hidden wicked nets, self-entrapment, ransom, and the full theological force of the Hebrew wordplay are not adequately established by the printed citation set alone.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
